et" edge moves. They both flip the internal disc D2, which is strategically sound as it doesn\'t give the opponent new frontier discs to play off of.\n\nReason: \n(C, 1) is the optimal move. Between the two edge moves, (C, 1) is safer because it is located on Row C, where the opponent currently has no pieces. In contrast, (E, 1) places a piece on Row E, where the opponent already has a presence (E4, E5), making the E1 piece more vulnerable to future flanking maneuvers (such as an opponent playing at E2). By playing (C, 1), I secure a stable edge anchor and restrict the opponent\'s mobility while keeping my pieces away from the opponent\'s main cluster.\n\nAnswer: (C, 1)'}
