mount of new "frontier" I provide the opponent.\n\nThere is B3 too. That move feels messy. It flips a couple of pieces, opens a few lines. Too complex to feel totally safe. G5 has possibilities, extending toward an edge. Still not worth the potential flip and risk of the board\'s shape changing into a direction I\'m not totally in control of. D7 is by far the most consistent.\n\nSo, I\'m going with D7. A well-calculated move that helps me establish control and doesn\'t give too much away.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the middle phase. I (\'@\') hold a solid position in the center-left and bottom-left, while the opponent (\'*\') occupies the upper-middle area. My pieces are well-connected, and I have several move options. The strategy should be to limit the opponent\'s mobility (minimize their available moves) by choosing "quiet moves" that flip few discs and avoid opening up corners or weak edges.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(D, 7)**: This is a very strong candidate. It is a "quiet move" that flips only one opponent disc (D6). It extends my control along row D towards the right edge without immediately taking the edge or exposing a corner. While it allows the opponent to potentially respond at (E, 7), the low number of flips preserves the board state and limits the opponent\'s options.\n- **(B, 3)**: A decent internal move, but it flips three discs (C2, C3, D3). Flipping C2 (a square diagonal to the corner) can be risky as it might eventually aid the opponent in accessing the corner A1.\n- **(A, 7)**: This move places a piece on a C-square (adjacent to a corner) on the edge. This is generally dangerous as it can give the opponent tactical chances to capture the corner A8.\n- **(E, 6)** and **(F, 6)**: These are reasonable centralizing moves but flip multiple discs (3 and 2 respectively), which increases the opponent\'s mobility more than (D, 7).\n\nReason: I choose (D, 7) because it is the best quiet move available. By flipping only a single disc, I minimize